WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.190
Begin with observations on the numbered terrain.

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:08.710
The broad plateau and southeast-trending volcanic chain are mostly basaltic, with

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:11.630
ages decreasing toward an active southeast end.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:15.310
That supports northwestward plate motion relative to a hot spot.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:20.490
The separate curved belt contains long-lived, explosive andesitic volcanoes beside a

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.450
trench, supporting an island arc.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:26.890
Similar volcanic landforms do not establish the same tectonic cause.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.890
Behind the arc, a low ridge with active basaltic eruptions and

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.450
offset segments marks back-arc spreading.

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:38.430
The western continent has a narrow shelf but no local trench,

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.230
active volcanism or earthquake evidence in the stated scenario.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.770
Its margin can therefore be passive despite its narrow width.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:49.570
Use all the observations; one visual clue should not override the

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:50.270
complete description.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.750
The central continent has a broad quiet shelf, canyons and sediment

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:59.490
piles. Its A to A-prime section shows normal-faulted continental crust buried

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:03.110
by Cenozoic sediment and grading toward oceanic crust.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:07.850
Farther east, a volcanic ridge separates young crustal age bands from

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:09.890
older crust near both continental edges.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:13.290
That pattern records an opening ocean bounded by passive margins.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:16.430
Use the actual ages to constrain the past.

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:19.950
An island now dated nineteen million years had not formed twenty

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.730
million years ago, whereas the forty-million-year plateau already existed.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:28.510
The oldest eastern-basin crust, about twenty to twenty-five million years, brackets

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:32.030
the basin's early opening. These ages constrain a reconstruction;

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.830
they do not provide exact plate speeds without distances and stated

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:36.190
assumptions.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.250
For the future, assume continuing motion explicitly.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:44.210
Spreading could widen the eastern ocean, the back-arc system could evolve,

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.690
and the plateau could approach the convergent boundary.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:51.550
Buoyant plateau material may accrete rather than disappear wholesale with dense

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:55.470
oceanic lithosphere. Use the complete numbered figure and the source descriptions,

00:01:55.470 --> 00:02:00.130
keep the missing tiny four-choice Connect geometry marked unavailable, and distinguish

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.190
observation, interpretation and prediction.
